docs[1]{path,scope}:
  README.md,project
close_reason: "Delivered by #141 \"Fix dependency projection using registered ordering semantics\" (merged as 79ab89e). Checks successful at the PR head 96c1c6e: CodeQL, codeql, semgrep-cloud-platform/scan, test (22), test (26)."
body: "## Reproduced shipped boundary\n\n- Official `pm-context@2026.10.10`, npm gitHead `49f1594f51e99f7306ccb7c9858287aa31765644`; downloaded tarball matches registry SHA-1 and SHA-512\n- Installed `@unbrained/pm-cli@2026.10.11`, Node `v24.19.0`, Linux\n- Public `buildContextPack`, `renderMarkdown`, `renderAgentHandoff`, and real installed `pm context-pack --include-deps` reproduce the association/implementation cases\n- Current main `22cc215d4a4f29525951e5b3c02c84c259a6918d` still has the same dependency projection statically. Its newer receipt work is source-only and was not executed in this review\n\n## Observed versus expected\n\nWith one stored edge from item `a` to item `b`:\n\n| Stored kind | Actual a.dependsOn | Expected a.dependsOn | Actual a.dependedBy | Expected a.dependedBy |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| related | b | empty | empty | empty |\n| implements | b | empty | empty | empty |\n| blocked_by | b | b | empty | empty |\n| blocks | b | empty | empty | b |\n\nThe positive `blocked_by` control passes. Both non-ordering controls and the inverse-ordering `blocks` control fail their intended assertions. All four raw `relationships[].kind` values remain correct. Markdown and agent renderers label the derived list literally as `a: depends_on [b]`, so it asserts ordering rather than merely showing all links.\n\nThe host's [dependency-kind contract](https://github.com/unbraind/pm-cli/blob/261a2e8f9ca235c42465bf26b8df2cbbc98d2228/docs/DEPENDENCY_KIND_CONTRACT.md) distinguishes association from ordering and defines `A --blocks--> B` as B waiting for A. The typed relationship registry is authoritative, including extension-defined semantics.\n\n## Minimal public-SDK reproduction\n\nIn a new empty directory with `pm-context@2026.10.10` and `@unbrained/pm-cli@2026.10.11` installed from npm, run this ESM script. No tracker, provider, or model is needed:\n\n```js\nimport { buildContextPack, renderAgentHandoff } from 'pm-context';\n\nfor (const kind of ['related', 'implements', 'blocked_by', 'blocks']) {\n  const pack = buildContextPack([\n    { id: 'a', title: 'Focus', type: 'Task', status: 'open',\n      dependencies: [{ id: 'b', kind }] },\n    { id: 'b', title: 'Context', type: 'Task', status: 'open' }\n  ], { ids: ['a'], includeDeps: true,\n       generatedAt: '2026-10-11T00:00:00.000Z' });\n  console.log(kind, JSON.stringify(pack.relationships), JSON.stringify(pack.deps));\n  console.log(renderAgentHandoff(pack));\n}\n```\n\nEvery case currently emits `a.dependsOn = ['b']`. The `related`/`implements` cases should retain their relationship and useful context without becoming scheduling dependencies. `blocks` must preserve the inverse scheduling direction.\n\nA real CLI review also reproduces this on a copied four-item synthetic tracker: a venue-selection task has `related` forecast evidence and an `implements` governing decision. `pm context-pack --id bf-work --include-body --include-deps --format json` retains those types in `relationships`, but reports both IDs in `deps[].dependsOn`. Agent output claims `bf-work: depends_on [bf-proof, bf-rule]`. There are no ordering edges in that fixture.\n\n## Cause and current source\n\n`buildContextPack` iterates every extracted relationship and inserts `rel.to` into `dependsOn[rel.from]` without consulting kind, ordering semantics, or inverse direction. Both renderers then print `depends_on`.\n\n- [Current projection, index.ts lines 573–592](https://github.com/unbraind/pm-context/blob/22cc215d4a4f29525951e5b3c02c84c259a6918d/index.ts#L573-L592)\n- [Markdown label, line 701](https://github.com/unbraind/pm-context/blob/22cc215d4a4f29525951e5b3c02c84c259a6918d/index.ts#L695-L704)\n- [Agent label, line 884](https://github.com/unbraind/pm-context/blob/22cc215d4a4f29525951e5b3c02c84c259a6918d/index.ts#L878-L887)\n\n## Impact and limits\n\nAn agent consuming the optional dependency summary can infer a prerequisite that is only evidence/association, or reverse who waits for whom. JSON still contains the accurate typed relationship list, and tracker files are not rewritten. This is a derived-output semantic defect, not demonstrated data loss, a provider defect, or a security vulnerability. Default full JSON without `--include-deps` completed the reviewed knowledge task correctly.\n\n## Existing owners and duplicate check\n\nAll-status GitHub issue/PR searches for `depends_on`, `relationship`, `related dependsOn`, and non-blocking terms found no exact defect report. All 74 canonical tracker items at the pinned main revision were indexed. Adjacent owners:\n\n- [PR 18: original include-deps addition](https://github.com/unbraind/pm-context/pull/18)\n- [pm-context-qbmf: neighborhood traversal](https://github.com/unbraind/pm-context/blob/22cc215d4a4f29525951e5b3c02c84c259a6918d/.agents/pm/features/pm-context-qbmf.toon)\n- [pm-context-opzn: agent handoff rendering](https://github.com/unbraind/pm-context/blob/22cc215d4a4f29525951e5b3c02c84c259a6918d/.agents/pm/features/pm-context-opzn.toon)\n- [pm-context-ci6v: usage receipts](https://github.com/unbraind/pm-context/blob/22cc215d4a4f29525951e5b3c02c84c259a6918d/.agents/pm/features/pm-context-ci6v.toon), which does not address this projection\n\n## Suggested acceptance\n\n1. Build ordering summaries from the public relationship semantics, preserving direction for `blocked_by`/`blocks` and declared extension kinds\n2. Retain non-ordering relationships and context with their correct kinds; do not drop them merely to fix the ordering summary\n3. Cover association-only, implementation-only, direct ordering, inverse ordering, and mixed cases through public builder plus real CLI JSON/Markdown/agent output\n4. Preserve source item/history bytes and existing raw typed relationships\n5. If the intended surface is all relationships rather than ordering, make the schema and rendered labels explicit about that instead of calling them `dependsOn`/`depends_on`\n\nNo implementation changes are included in this report."
